cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use the proof, its mathematical compression route, the tooling or the measurements, please cite this paper-repository (see also paper/references.bib)."
title: "Why It Fits: The Mathematical Structure of a 2,931-Token Lean 4 Proof of the Classical Zero-Free Region"
abstract: "A research paper (English and Chinese) explaining the mathematics behind a record-breaking compressed Lean 4 proof of the classical de la Vallee Poussin zero-free region for the Riemann zeta function. The proof replaces four re-derived analytic components by library-native structure: a Mellin entire formula for (s-1)zeta(s) that yields both the growth bound and a cheap zero exclusion near s=1; a recursive zero-peeling lemma built from analytic divided differences (dslope) and Borel-Caratheodory, replacing finite zero products and contour estimates; a von Mangoldt bridge into the classical 3-4-1 inequality; and an adaptive scale a = Re s + 5d with d = 2^-20/log R that makes every estimate crude yet decisive. The whole proof is one file of 2,931 normalized tokens versus a 21,641-token / 21-file reference (86.5% reduction); official leaderboard submission 96283 has score 65.1339 with proof-compression curve flag kept=true. The paper includes component-level token accounting, an ablation showing the Euler-Maclaurin growth route costs ~50% more formal tokens, and the published technique note core1_borel_dslope reproduced in both languages."
